TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"2.2 Launch"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"2.5 Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit"},{"id":"R10","met":"yes","where":"Appendix A: STK files and settings"},{"id":"R11","met":"yes","where":"Throughout report"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"2.3 Orbit","issue":"The approximately five-day revisit estimate divides equatorial ground-track spacing by maximum roll-reach width. It does not demonstrate actual access to every reef location under the selected imaging, lighting, cloud, and pointing constraints; treat it as an approximate geometric estimate."},{"where":"2.1 Spacecraft overview","issue":"The 5 m GSD and 60 km swath are derived from pixel count and focal-plane geometry, but no radiometric/SNR, detector, or optical system performance analysis establishes that the proposed 0.10 m aperture can deliver usable multispectral reef imagery."},{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The report acknowledges that no X-band link budget has been calculated. Thus the assumed 50 Mbit/s rate and resulting daily downlink margin are not verified for the stated spacecraft, antenna, and station."}],"overall":"A strong, well-organised preliminary mission design with a clear Australian benefit, substantial orbital and operations analysis, and useful STK evidence. The main shortcomings are unverified imaging and communications performance and simplified coverage estimates; these should be resolved before treating the stated mission capability as demonstrated.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":9.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Clearly states the mission, explains the need and rationale, justifies a single spacecraft, and identifies the operator and customer groups. The rationale is specific and supported with citations, although the cited evidence and mission-performance case could be more rigorously substantiated.","evidence":[{"where":"1 Introduction","quote":"The primary mission of ZYZZsat is to image the whole Great Barrier Reef in visible bands at 5 m resolution"},{"where":"1 Introduction","quote":"One satellite is therefore used."},{"where":"1 Introduction","quote":"The operator is the Australian Institute of Marine Science (AIMS)"},{"where":"1 Introduction","quote":"The customers are the Great Barrier Reef Marine Park Authority"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":48.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"Comprehensive preliminary design with launch alternatives, orbital elements and J2 sun-synchronous calculation, STK figures/access results, communications/data budget, and a reasoned EOL concept. Strong evidence overall. The link budget is explicitly absent, performance feasibility is not fully demonstrated, and the revisit estimates rely on simplified geometry rather than reported mission coverage analysis.","evidence":[{"where":"2.2 Launch","quote":"The baseline is an SSMS rideshare flight on Vega C from Kourou in early 2025"},{"where":"2.3 Orbit","quote":"The orbit is circular, so it has no perigee and two of the six classical orbital elements, the argument of perigee and the true anomaly, are undefined."},{"where":"2.3 Orbit","quote":"No orbital manoeuvres are required."},{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications","quote":"There are six passes each day, three in the evening and three in the morning (UTC)"},{"where":"2.5 Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"The mission lasts five years."},{"where":"Appendix A: STK files and settings","quote":"The following STK 11 files are submitted with this report in ZYZZsat_STK.zip."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Draws specific conclusions from the proposed orbit, coverage, communications, and EOL analysis, and identifies important limitations and next steps. Conclusions are relevant rather than generic, though some claimed performance remains preliminary.","evidence":[{"where":"3 Conclusion","quote":"The design has four limitations."},{"where":"3 Conclusion","quote":"With these points addressed in the next design phase, the mission can provide the direct, regular bleaching maps that current temperature-based monitoring cannot."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":16.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Clear report structure, executive summary, labelled figures and tables, generally professional writing, and a bibliography corresponding to in-text citations. Some source entries are incomplete or less authoritative than ideal, and key engineering assumptions (notably imaging feasibility and link performance) are not supported by detailed evidence.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"This report presents the design of ZYZZsat (Z-axis/Y-axis Zeroed Zoning Satellite), a single Earth observation microsatellite"},{"where":"2 Mission Design","quote":"The mission is described in five parts: the spacecraft, launch, orbit, ground control and communications, and mission duration and end of life (EOL)."},{"where":"2.3 Orbit","quote":"Figure 1: STK 3D view of the ZYZZsat orbit at two times 12 hours apart (generated in STK 11)."},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[11] D. A. Vallado, Fundamentals of Astrodynamics and Applications, 4th ed."}]}]}
